<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.7.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01374</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.7.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ddee1421-0b69-4ede-9041-0bd6ad4fe983</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <exercise id="a5ee2468-69c0-4130-985e-a3f0c94a4142" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">The two graphs show models characterized by exponential decay representing the area covered by two different algae blooms, in square yards, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> weeks after different chemicals were applied.</para>
          
          <para id="para-00002">(<media alt="Graph of two functions on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_01.svg"/></media>)</para>
          <para id="para-00003">Which algae bloom covered a larger area when the chemicals were applied?</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00005">The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve has a higher <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve has a lower <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00007">Both algae blooms covered the same area after 5 weeks.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve is always below the other one on the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00009">Correct! The algae bloom represented by the triangles because it has a higher <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">The correct answer is: The algae bloom represented by the triangles because it has a higher <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_08f2e010-a77c-447b-a9b7-796d79040bf5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">The two graphs show models characterized by exponential decay representing the area covered by two different algae blooms, in square yards, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> weeks after different chemicals were applied.</para>
          
          <para id="para-00013">(<media alt="Graph of two functions on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_02.svg"/></media>)</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Which algae population is decreasing more rapidly? Explain how you know.</para>
            <para id="para-00015">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve has a higher <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">Both algae blooms are decreasing at the same rate.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve is always below the other one on the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00019">The algae bloom represented by the triangles because its graph is steeper.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00020">Correct! The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curves graph is steeper.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00022">The correct answer is the algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve's graph is steeper.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a89b1e97-30fc-428f-baba-62b0d7a9180b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00023">A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.</para>
          
          <para id="para-00024">(<media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_03.svg"/></media>)</para>
          <para id="para-00025">What fraction of the burn area remains each week?</para>
            <para id="para-00026">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00027"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>.5</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00028"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>.75</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00029"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>.25</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00030"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>.66</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00031">Correct! .75 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00032">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">The correct answer is .75 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="dd028626-75d1-412e-80f9-2bb42d1333b5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00034">A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.</para>
          
          <para id="para-00035">(
            <media alt="Graph of a function on grid.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_04.svg"/>
          </media>
          )
          </para>
          <para id="para-00036">Which of the following is an equation representing the area of the burn, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> weeks?</para>
            <para id="para-00037">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00038"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00039"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00040"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00041"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00042">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00043">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00044">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_816acdc7-11e3-4a82-8f9a-c533c67578ad" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00045">A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.</para>
          
          <para id="para-00046">(<media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_05.svg"/></media>)</para>
          <para id="para-00047">What is the area of the burn after 7 weeks? Round to three decimal places.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00048">Correct! 1.068</para>
            <para id="para-00049">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00050">The correct answer is 1.068.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_558b3142-705a-46fe-bd11-b132fffc0fc8" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00051">The graphs show the amounts of medicine in two patients after receiving injections. The circles show the medicine in patient A, and the triangles show the medicine in patient B.</para>
          
          <para id="para-00052">(<media alt="Two functions plotted on the same grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_06.svg"/></media>)</para>
          <para id="para-00053">One equation that gives the amount of medicine in milligrams, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in patient A, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> hours after an injection, is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>300</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00054">What could be an equation for the amount of medicine in patient B?</para>
            <para id="para-00055">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00056"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>500</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00057"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>500</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00058"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00059"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00060">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00061">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00062">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c0ce2bd6-b9f8-487b-8877-095fa40740a4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00063">Which scenario could describe the graph?</para>

          <para id="para-00064">(<media alt="GRAPH OF AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 10 AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (1, 20) AND (2, 40)."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_07.jpg"/></media>)</para>
            <para id="para-00065">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00066">A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria.</para>
            <para id="para-00067">A savings account starts with $200 and decays by a factor of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> each month.</para>
            <para id="para-00068">A population of bacteria increases by a factor of 10 every hour and starts with 2 bacteria.</para>
            <para id="para-00069">A population of bacteria starts with 200 and decays by a factor of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> every hour.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00070">Correct! A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria.</para>
            <para id="para-00071">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">The correct answer is: A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a1f8b0b4-7b47-4e6b-9bc9-229400a0148c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00073">Which equation could represent the graph?</para>
          <para id="para-00074">(<media alt="SCATTER PLOT THAT CAN BE MODELED BY AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts THAT IS SLIGHTLY MORE THAN 2. THE SCATTER PLOT INCLUDES A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 1 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 3-POINT-1, A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 2 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 4-POINT-6, AND A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 3 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 6-POINT-8."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_08.jpg"/></media>)</para>
            <para id="para-00075">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00076"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.104</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.479</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00077"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00078"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.104</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>.479</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00079"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>.479</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2.104</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            
            <para id="para-00080">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00081">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.104</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.479</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1b655f00-f67d-49bb-9eca-e83983e2de0d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00082">Given a formula for an exponential function, is it possible to determine whether the function grows or decays exponentially just by looking at the formula? Explain.</para>
            <para id="para-00083">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Yes, if the initial value is greater than one it’s growth; if the initial value is less than one it’s decay.</para>
            <para id="para-00085">Yes, if the growth rate is between zero and one it’s decay; if the growth rate is greater than one it is growth.</para>
            <para id="para-00086">Yes, if the growth rate is greater than one it’s growth; if the growth rate is, less than zero it’s decay.</para>
            <para id="para-00087">No, it is not possible to determine growth and decay from the formula.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            
            <para id="para-00088">Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00089">The correct answer is: Yes, if the growth rate is between zero and one, it’s decay; if the growth rate is greater than one, it’s growth.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
